 of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =P(X\\in \\{4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.6)^{4}(0.4)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.6)^{5}(0.4)^{5-5} \\\\  & =5(0.6)^{4}(0.4)^{1}+1(0.6)^{5}(0.4)^{0} \\\\  & =0.337\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426426 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426426 #edb/ref 1069 "q-279687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426426 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426426 #edb/ref 1071 "If $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X>2)≈${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426426 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2083b042-5bf1-5223-9478-ae910fe13957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426426 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-4} \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}+1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-279695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426427 #edb/ref 1071 "If $X∼B(4,0.3),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<2)≈${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75d3c9e3-0aae-5212-8d84-2a70ca742fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426427 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(0.3)}^{x}{(0.7)}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(0.3)}^{0}{(0.7)}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(0.3)}^{1}{(0.7)}^{4-1} \\\\  & =1{(0.3)}^{0}{(0.7)}^{4}+4{(0.3)}^{1}{(0.7)}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.652\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426428 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426428 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426428 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426428 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $X∼B(8,0.3),$ compute $P(X>2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426428 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b719cf3-bd28-5a38-b0fa-b59090fe757c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426428 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(8,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{8-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X>2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=3,4,…,8.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\le 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0})(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{8-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{8-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{8-2} \\\\  & =(0.7)^{8}+8(0.3)(0.7)^{7}+28(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5518\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5518 \\\\  & =0.4482\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426429 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(3\\le X\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a8ce807-1d45-5332-844a-554a40b8b81d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426429 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{1} \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426430 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426430 #edb/ref 1069 "q-279595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426430 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426430 #edb/ref 1071 "If $X∼B(3,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8dd92dbf-67ea-5e4b-a006-5fb0637bcddd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426430 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3-1} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426431 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $X∼B(5,0.5),$ compute $P(X>3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a63141b0-e3e5-58fd-a59c-49398986fa85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426431 #edb/ref 1074 "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-